  "question": {
    "text": "A particle of mass $m$ is under the influence of the gravitational field of a body of mass $M(\\gg m)$. The particle is moving in a circular orbit of radius $r_0$ with time period $T_0$ around the mass $M$. Then, the particle is subjected to an additional central force, corresponding to the potential energy $V_{\\mathrm{c}}(r)=m \\alpha / r^3$, where $\\alpha$ is a positive constant of suitable dimensions and $r$ is the distance from the center of the orbit. If the particle moves in the same circular orbit of radius $r_0$ in the combined gravitational potential due to $M$ and $V_{\\mathrm{c}}(r)$, but with a new time period $T_1$, then $\\left(T_1^2-T_0^2\\right) / T_1^2$ is given by\n[ $G$ is the gravitational constant.]",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "TqrGL",
      "text": "$\\frac{3 \\alpha}{G M r_0^2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ASV8Q",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha}{2 G M r_0^2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "HnbnG",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\alpha}{G M r_0^2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "RO4sW",
      "text": "$\\frac{2 \\alpha}{G M r_0^2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{F}_1=\\frac{\\mathrm{GMm}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^2} \\\\ & \\mathrm{~F}_2=\\frac{\\mathrm{GMm}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^2}-\\frac{3 \\mathrm{~m} \\alpha}{\\mathrm{r}_0^4} \\\\ & \\frac{\\omega_1^2}{\\omega_0^2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{F}_2}{\\mathrm{~F}_1}=\\frac{\\frac{\\mathrm{GM}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^2}-\\frac{3 \\alpha}{\\mathrm{r}_0^4}}{\\frac{\\mathrm{GM}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^2}} \\\\ & \\frac{\\mathrm{T}_0^2}{\\mathrm{~T}_1^2}=1-\\frac{3 \\alpha}{\\mathrm{GMr}_0^2} \\\\ & \\frac{\\mathrm{T}_1^2-\\mathrm{T}_0^2}{\\mathrm{~T}_1^2}=\\frac{3 \\alpha}{\\mathrm{GMr}_0^2}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
